First, I should probably check the current IP configuration on h2. That way, I can see what interfaces are available and what their current IP addresses are. Using the "ip" tool without any arguments should give me a summary of all network interfaces. This is a good starting point because if I don't know the current setup, I can't make informed changes.

Once I have the IP information, the next logical step would be to test connectivity to the other nodes. Starting with h1 makes sense since it's the first in the list. Sending a ping request to h1 will tell me if there's a route available and if packets are getting through. If the ping is successful, that means h2 can reach h1, which is a good sign. If not, I'll need to troubleshoot why the connection is failing—maybe there's a firewall blocking ICMP requests, or the routing tables aren't set up correctly.

After verifying connectivity with h1, I should perform the same check with h3. It's important to ensure that h2 can communicate with both other nodes independently. If one connection works and the other doesn't, I'll need to investigate the specific issues related to h3. Perhaps there's a different subnet involved, or maybe the interface connecting h2 to h3 isn't configured properly.

If any of the ping tests fail, I'll need to look into why that's happening. Checking the routing table with the "route" tool could help identify if there's a missing route to the destination node. Additionally, verifying firewall settings to ensure they're not blocking ICMP or the necessary ports is crucial. Sometimes, even if the network is set up correctly, firewalls can prevent communication between nodes.

Another thing to consider is whether all nodes are on the same subnet. If h1 and h3 are on different subnets, h2 might need a default gateway or specific routes to reach them. Configuring the routing table appropriately would be necessary in that case. Alternatively, if the network uses dynamic routing protocols like OSPF or RIP, ensuring that they're properly configured and running on all nodes would help in automatic route discovery.

Documentation and logging are also important. Keeping track of the commands run and the results obtained will help in diagnosing any issues that arise later. If something goes wrong after making changes, having a record of previous states can be invaluable in troubleshooting.

